Let's consider a simple example. Suppose A is a 2x2 matrix with the following matrix:

A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{bmatrix}

We can find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of A. The characteristic equation of A is det(A - λI) = 0, which gives us:

\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} 1-\lambda & 2 \\ 3 & 4-\lambda \end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\
(1-\lambda)(4-\lambda) - 6 &= 0 \\
\lambda^2 - 5\lambda - 2 &= 0 \\
(\lambda - 6)(\lambda + 1) &= 0
\end{align*}

The eigenvalues of A are λ = 6 and λ = -1. Now, let's find the eigenvectors corresponding to these eigenvalues.

For λ = 6, we have:

\begin{align*}
\begin{bmatrix} 1-6 & 2 \\ 3 & 4-6 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} &= \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \\
\begin{bmatrix} -5 & 2 \\ 3 & -2 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} &= \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}

This implies that the eigenvector corresponding to λ = 6 is \begin{bmatrix} -5 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}.

